course: dictionary
lesson: 8188
title: Restart: starting again
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To restart means to start something again after it has stopped. You can restart a game, a journey, or a lesson. The clue is the little word re-, which means again.
explain: «Restarting really does fix many glitches, because it clears out a computer's muddle.» Here restart means switch the computer off and on again, so it can begin fresh. The sentence also tells you that restarting does not fix everything.
explain: Restart can be a verb, as in «restart the game», or a noun, as in «a fresh restart». When you meet it, try putting in the words start again. If the sentence still makes sense, you have found its meaning.
fact: Restart is a verb: restart, restarts, restarted, restarting. It can also be a noun: a restart.
fact: To restart means to start something again after it has stopped.
fact: To restart a computer or phone means to switch it off and on again.
fact: A glitch is a small fault that makes a computer or game behave strangely.
fact: Restarting often fixes a frozen device, but it can't fix a cracked screen or a broken part.
exercise: «Restarting really does fix many glitches.» What does restarting a computer mean? | To pick up a new keyboard | *To switch it off and on again | To make it louder | To hide the screen
exercise: A game stops after a goal and then begins again from the start. What is the game doing? | It is ending for ever | It is breaking | *It is restarting | It is paused for lunch
exercise: Mia's tablet froze, so she decided to restart it. What will she do? | Sing a song to it | Throw it out of the window | *Switch it off and on again | Wait for a week
test: «Restarting doesn't fix everything: it can't fix a cracked screen or a broken part.» What can a restart not fix? | *A cracked screen | A frozen app | A computer that is slow and muddled
test: Which word is the verb in «The team restarted the match after the rain stopped»? | rain | *restarted | after | team
exam: What does restart mean? | To stop something for ever | To make something longer | To start something for the first time | *To start something again after it has stopped
